House cats are descended from wild hunters, so even today their instincts are shaped by the small prey animals they historically hunted. Here are the most common foods (prey) house cats used to hunt:

🐭 Small Mammals

Mice (primary and most common prey)

Rats (especially young or small rats)

Voles

Shrews

Moles

Rabbits (mostly kittens/young rabbits)

Chipmunks

Squirrels (usually juveniles)

🐦 Birds

Small songbirds (sparrows, finches)

Ground-nesting birds

Baby birds or fledglings

Pigeons and doves (young or weak individuals)

🦎 Reptiles & Amphibians

Lizards

Geckos

Small snakes

Frogs

Toads

🐜 Invertebrates

Insects (grasshoppers, crickets, beetles)

Spiders

Moths

Flies

🐟 Other Prey (Less Common)

Fish (from shallow water or ponds)

Crayfish

Small turtles (very rare)

Important Notes

Cats are obligate carnivores, evolved to eat whole prey (meat, organs, bones).

Hunting is instinctual, even when cats are well-fed.

Domestic cats typically hunt small, easy-to-catch animals rather than large prey.

Outdoor hunting can pose risks (parasites, toxins, wildlife impact).

